<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  rawpage.exe's application manifest. ONE setting, and it is load-bearing.

  THE SEGMENT HEAP (Stage 2 issue I-35). Without this, every allocation through msvcrt (our code,
  Qt, every tile's pixel buffer) comes from the classic NT heap. Tiles are ~256 KB each, allocated
  and freed by the thousand between long-lived small blocks, and the classic heap kept the freed
  memory committed in thousands of gaps it could neither reuse nor return: measured through the real
  single-instance pipe at 60 -> 167 MB of committed-but-free memory in msvcrt's heap over 30 rounds of
  ten documents, while live memory was flat. The segment heap allocates blocks above 128 KB as whole
  pages and decommits them on free: the same workload held private bytes flat (73.1 -> 72.2 MB), and
  A2's render figure got FASTER (p95 13.7-14.0 ms against 14.5-15.3 ms, same session).

  Honoured from Windows 10 2004 (build 19041). The product supports 1703 and later (app/main.cpp);
  on 1703-1909 the element is ignored and the process keeps the classic heap and I-35's residual.

  NO DOUBLE HYPHEN ANYWHERE IN THIS COMMENT: XML forbids it inside a comment, Windows then rejects the
  whole manifest, and the executable fails to start with a side-by-side configuration error. That is
  what the first version of this file did; tests/platform/tst_manifest.cpp now launches the product.

  NOT a place for DPI awareness: that is set by API in app/main.cpp, which measures it, and a second
  source of the same setting is a disagreement waiting to happen.
-->
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <heapType xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2020/WindowsSettings">SegmentHeap</heapType>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
